In addition, and specifically for this domain: Scriptura is research
infrastructure. It aggregates, structures, cross-references and analyses
source material. Inclusion of a text, translation, lexicon, commentary,
annotation, or interpretation in Scriptura does not constitute endorsement of
its accuracy, authority, canonicity, or doctrinal position by the project or by
any contributor.

Scriptura deliberately and permanently separates five epistemic layers, and no
tooling, query, report, or agent output may collapse them:

  * scripture                  - text of a witness, as attested by a named edition
  * historical_evidence        - material and documentary evidence external to scripture
  * linguistic_observation     - morphology, syntax, lexicography, philology
  * theological_interpretation - interpretive positions of named traditions and authors
  * ai_hypothesis              - machine-generated inference, always flagged as non-authoritative

This separation is normative, not advisory. It is specified in
docs/architecture/BKB-DATA-001, enforced by src/scriptura/core/layers.py, and
governed by .clinerules/020-epistemic-layers.md.

